{"created": "2022-06-26T21:04:14.711253+00:00", "modified": "2024-07-16T13:17:04.487559+00:00", "id": 6757507, "conceptrecid": "6757506", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.6757506", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "metadata": {"title": "The Stellivore Hypothesis: An Exploration with Gaia DR3", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "publication_date": "2022-06-26", "description": "<p><strong>&nbsp;Abstract:</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; The stellivore hypothesis (Vidal 2014, Chap 9; Vidal 2016; Vidal 2020) proposes to reinterpret some known accreting binary star systems as Type II civilizations. A stellivore can be seen as a variation on the Dyson sphere idea, making the most of accretion power, instead of solar collectors.&nbsp;<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Since it was proposed, no clear refutation or confirmation has been advanced. What lacks are concrete predictions and data supporting them.<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Drawing on Gaia DR3 data, I present preliminary results of apparent goal-directed motions of stellivore candidates in the galactic field. Uncertainties remain, but I discuss how these could be reduced and mitigated.<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Goal-directedness is a hallmark of intentionality and intelligence, which, if proven with enough statistical significance would be thought-provoking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>References: </strong></p>\n\n<p>Vidal, C. 2014. The Beginning and the End - The Meaning of Life in a Cosmological Perspective. Frontiers Collection. Springer. <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1301.1648\">http://arxiv.org/abs/1301.1648</a>.<br>\n&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;. 2016. &ldquo;Stellivore Extraterrestrials? Binary Stars as Living Systems.&rdquo; Acta Astronautica 128: 251&ndash;56. doi:10.1016/j.actaastro.2016.06.038. <a href=\"https://zenodo.org/record/164853\">https://zenodo.org/record/164853</a>.<br>\n&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;. 2020. &ldquo;Energy Rate Density as a Technosignature: The Case for Stellivores.&rdquo; Presented at Life in the Universe: Big History, SETI and the Future of Humankind. IBHA &amp; INAF-IASF MI Symposium 15-16 July 2019. <a href=\"http://www.clemvidal.com/s/Vidal-2020-ERD-as-a-technosignature-case-for-stellivores.pdf\">http://www.clemvidal.com/s/Vidal-2020-ERD-as-a-technosignature-case-for-stellivores.pdf</a>.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Cl\u00e9ment Vidal", "affiliation": "University of California Berkeley", "orcid": "0000-0001-6689-5570"}], "keywords": ["stellivore", "gaia", "stellar dynamics", "technosignature", "pulsars: spider"], "version": "3", "resource_type": {"title": "Poster", "type": "poster"}, "meeting": {"title": "The First Penn State SETI Symposium", "acronym": "PSETI Symposium", "dates": "27-30 June 2022", "place": "State College, PA", "url": "https://sites.psu.edu/setisymposium2022/"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "communities": [{"id": "pseti-symposium-2022"}], "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "6757506"}}]}}, "title": "The Stellivore Hypothesis: An Exploration with Gaia DR3", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/6757507", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/6757507", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/6757507?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.6757507", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/6757507/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/6757506", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/6757506", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6757506", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.6757506", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:6757507/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:6757507/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/6757507/files", "media_files": "ht